## What happened

**`DSA.Create()` throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` on macOS** because Apple's Security.framework has never natively supported DSA. In .NET 10 and earlier, the runtime shipped an OpenSSL-based fallback on macOS for DSA. In .NET 11, that fallback was removed as part of the ongoing DSA deprecation (DSA was already marked `[Obsolete]` with **SYSLIB0040** since .NET 7).

On **Linux** it still works (uses OpenSSL directly). On **Windows** it still works (uses CNG).

## How to fix it

**Short-term** — use **BouncyCastle** (or another managed crypto library) for DSA verification on macOS:

```bash
dotnet add package BouncyCastle.Cryptography
```

```csharp
using Org.BouncyCastle.Crypto.Parameters;
using Org.BouncyCastle.Security;

// Platform-agnostic DSA signature verification
public static bool VerifyDsa(byte[] publicKeyBytes, byte[] data, byte[] signature)
{
    var keyParam = (DsaPublicKeyParameters)PublicKeyFactory.CreateKey(publicKeyBytes);
    var verifier = SignerUtilities.GetSigner("SHA-1withDSA"); // or SHA256withDSA
    verifier.Init(false, keyParam);
    verifier.BlockUpdate(data, 0, data.Length);
    return verifier.VerifySignature(signature);
}
```

**Long-term** — migrate away from DSA entirely. DSA is considered legacy; prefer **ECDSA** or **EdDSA** for new signatures, and re-sign any artifacts that currently rely on DSA verification.
